  "final_text": "I checked the report's numbers against the raw CSVs. My answer is **no, not yet. Rework CRAFT first.** The VP's two arguments, demand growth and improving quality, both come from how the metrics are defined. Neither holds up on a like-for-like basis.\n\n## The two claims, checked\n\n**1. \"Units up 22.4% on August\"**\n- **What's counted:** CRAFT units shipped, from `demand_actuals.csv` (`units_actual`, rows where the SKU line is CRAFT).\n- **Why it misleads:** it compares month totals of unequal length. August has 4 weekly buckets (Aug 5–26). September has 5 (Sep 2–30), and the 5th, Sep 30, is actually labelled FY24-Q3-W01. The report says this itself in §6.\n- **Raw data check:** August = 7,488 and September = 9,162, matching the report's table in §2.\n\n| Basis | Aug | Sep | Change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Month total | 7,488 | 9,162 | **+22.4%** |\n| Per week | 1,872 | 1,832 | **−2.1%** |\n| Same 4 weeks (Sep 2–23) | 7,488 | 7,127 | **−4.8%** |\n\nDemand for CRAFT did not grow. The extra week accounts for all of the increase.\n\n**2. \"Defects fell from 3.21 to 2.51 per 1,000 units\"**\n- **What's counted:** the number of defect reports (NCRs) in `ncr_records.csv`, divided by units shipped.\n- **Why it misleads:** the number of reports barely moved (24 → 23). The rate dropped because the extra week added units to the denominator. Each NCR can cover anywhere from 75 to 970 units, so counting reports says little about how much product was actually defective.\n- **Raw data check:** 23 / 9,162 × 1,000 = 2.51. This matches §3.\n\n| Metric | Aug (4 wks) | Sep (5 wks) | Sep 2–23 (4 wks) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| NCRs per 1,000 units (report's definition) | 3.21 | 2.51 | 3.09 |\n| Units affected, as % of units shipped | 31.0% | 30.9% | **38.7%** |\n| Units scrapped | 924 | 1,539 | **1,464 (+58%)** |\n\nCounted by quantity, CRAFT quality got worse, not better. More units were affected and scrap rose sharply. The week that makes September look better (Sep 30) has only 1 NCR. It is also the newest week, so its defect reports may not be complete yet; I can't confirm that from the files. The VP's line \"no other line improved\" is also off: PULSE was flat (0.70 → 0.71).\n\n**Caveat on the % metric:** the defect feed tracks *production* problems, while the denominator is units *sold*. So \"31% affected\" is a rough measure of defect intensity, not a return rate or a share of output (§6). Even so, CRAFT is far worse than the other lines on the same basis: AURA 5.8%, PULSE 8.5%, NEST 10.6%.\n\n## Recommendation\n- **Decision:** don't move Q3 marketing to CRAFT now. Put the money and attention into rework first.\n- **Why:** like-for-like, CRAFT is getting fewer orders, not more (−2.1% per week). Its defect volume is flat to rising, and scrap is up 58% over the same 4 weeks. More demand means more production on a line where roughly 30–39% of shipped volume has defect reports against it.\n- **Risk of waiting:** if CRAFT demand is real, we lose some premium sales during the rework period.\n- **Risk of spending now:** more defective production and scrap on the line with the worst quality, and a premium brand exposed to more customers.\n- **Gaps:** there are no cost figures, so CRAFT's €4.12M is revenue at list price, not margin (§6). Marketing budgets can't be attributed to individual lines (§4), so the effect of extra spend can't be measured either.\n- **What would change my answer** (my proposed thresholds, not targets set by the team): for 4 consecutive full weeks, CRAFT units affected stay below about 10% of units shipped (roughly NEST's level, versus 31–39% today), weekly scrap stays below about 150 units (versus about 230 in August), and per-week CRAFT demand is at least stable. These are set in units of product, not report counts, so merging or splitting reports can't move them.\n\nThere were no team metric definitions to check against, so the definitions above are mine. If Quality defines its defect rate officially as reports per 1,000 units, it's worth deciding together whether that should change. Under that definition, CRAFT can look like it's improving while the amount of defective product goes up.",
